    "body": "> I agree that Firefox is correct in this case. Could you file this at [crbug.com](crbug.com)?\r\n\r\nI wouldn't be hurry in that case because, because reason is below...\r\n\r\nI played with this demo a little bit more and figure out more interesting thing: some events in these browsers are treated by different ways.\r\nTake my demo and change \"_click_\" event onto these events: _mouseenter, mouseout, mouseover, mouseleave_. You could see that in that case behaviour between chrome and firefox has changed. And now it looks like firefox works wrong, but not chrome, it's correct. But if we take these events: _mouseup, mousedown_ - you'll see that and chrome and firefox works same and they are both incorrect. By the way, try event _mousemove_, and you'll see that behaviour same as \"_click_\".\r\nNow, I've really confused up. Now I don't understand what is going on here... \r\n\r\n@jakearchibald after that do you still agree with that firefox is wrong? Are you sure what I should create an issue on crbug?",
